<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Krbava Field</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1493">1493</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-ottoman-wars">Croatian–Ottoman wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Krbava Field was fought on 9 September 1493 on the Krbava plain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lika">Lika</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, between a Croatian army under Ban Emerik Derenčin and Ottoman raiders led by Hadım Yakub Pasha. The Croatian nobility suffered a heavy defeat: the ban was captured and many nobles were killed. The disaster opened Croatia to further Ottoman raids.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-krbava-field.jpg" alt="Battle of Krbava Field" width="320" height="360" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Leonhard Beck · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Krbava_Field.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the fall of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1463, raids by the Akıncı, irregular light cavalry, grew more frequent. They plundered the countryside in spring and summer and avoided fortified towns, driving the local population from the land. A raid of September 1491 ended when the Croatian ban Ladislav of Egervár and Count Bernardin Frankopan defeated the Ottomans at the Battle of Vrpile, and the raids stopped in 1492. They resumed once Hadım Yakup Pasha became sanjak-bey of Bosnia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In the summer of 1493 Hadım Yakup Pasha raised 8,000 light cavalry, failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jajce">Jajce</a>, and raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>. A war between the new ban of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, Emerik Derenčin, and the Frankopans over Senj was set aside because of the Ottoman raid. The Croatian nobles gathered about 3,000 cavalry and 8,000 infantry, part of them peasants, to meet the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> returning from sacking Modruš. Ivan Frankopan Cetinski urged an ambush in the ravines, but Derenčin accused the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> of cowardice and chose open battle on the Krbava plain.</p><p>On 9 September the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> feigned a retreat and drew the Croatian army towards a hidden force of about 3,000 cavalry in the woods, which struck its rear while the main Ottoman army attacked the front. Most of the Croatian infantry was surrounded and only a few reached the fortified town of Udbina. The battle lasted from about nine in the morning into the afternoon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Derenčin was captured and died in captivity, and his brother and son Pavao were killed. Ivan Frankopan Cetinski, Petar II Zrinski and Juraj Vlatković were among the nobles killed. Nikola VI Frankopan was ransomed, and Bernardin Frankopan and Franjo Berislavić survived. Older historians called the defeat the first dissolution of the Croatian Kingdom, and local tradition named the plain the Field of Blood.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> made no territorial gains from the victory, and a peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> was signed in April 1495. In the following decades, under constant attack, the Ottomans gradually expanded into southern Croatia. Older sources exaggerate the numbers involved, since the reports were written under the impression of the heavy loss.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Krbava Field” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
